{
  "note": "The Lateral Geniculate Nucleus",
  "source": "7ba51b53d79f",
  "script": "c1b4fc27b568",
  "voice": "Patrick - Engaging and Warm",
  "model": "eleven_v4",
  "fingerprint": "f8a2b83612fb",
  "duration": 184.163,
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "The Lateral Geniculate Nucleus",
      "start": 0,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "The lateral geniculate nucleus, or L G N, is the thalamic relay of the visual pathway.",
          "start": 0,
          "end": 6.16
        },
        {
          "text": "It receives ninety per cent of retinal axons, and it sends the signal on to the visual cortex.",
          "start": 6.2,
          "end": 12.08
        },
        {
          "text": "The nucleus is organised into six layers of cell bodies.",
          "start": 12.12,
          "end": 15.76
        },
        {
          "text": "These layers keep the inputs of the two eyes, and of the different ganglion cell types, strictly segregated.",
          "start": 15.84,
          "end": 21.787
        },
        {
          "text": "We'll look first at the map the L G N holds, and then at its six layers and where they project.",
          "start": 21.813,
          "end": 27.68
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Retinotopic organisation",
      "start": 28.447,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "The first thing to know is that the L G N holds a retinotopic map.",
          "start": 28.447,
          "end": 32.967
        },
        {
          "text": "That means neighbouring points on the retina project to neighbouring points in the nucleus.",
          "start": 33.007,
          "end": 37.927
        },
        {
          "text": "So the spatial order of the visual field is preserved at this relay.",
          "start": 37.967,
          "end": 42.287
        },
        {
          "text": "That said, the map is heavily distorted in favour of central vision.",
          "start": 42.367,
          "end": 46.647
        },
        {
          "text": "Fifty per cent of the representation is the fovea.",
          "start": 46.687,
          "end": 49.687
        },
        {
          "text": "This mirrors the dominance of the fovea in visual acuity.",
          "start": 49.727,
          "end": 53.407
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "The six layers",
      "start": 54.178,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "Now for the structure itself.",
          "start": 54.178,
          "end": 56.418
        },
        {
          "text": "The L G N is built from six layers of cell bodies, separated by intralaminar layers.",
          "start": 56.498,
          "end": 62.218
        },
        {
          "text": "These intralaminar layers are also called koniocellular layers, and they take the input from K cells.",
          "start": 62.258,
          "end": 68.685
        },
        {
          "text": "The six layers are numbered one to six, going from ventral to dorsal.",
          "start": 68.711,
          "end": 73.018
        },
        {
          "text": "Each layer receives input from one eye only, and from one ganglion cell type only.",
          "start": 73.058,
          "end": 78.498
        },
        {
          "text": "Because of that, the numbering tells you exactly which input sits where.",
          "start": 78.578,
          "end": 83.111
        },
        {
          "text": "Layers one and two are the magnocellular layers, which receive input from M cells.",
          "start": 83.245,
          "end": 88.298
        },
        {
          "text": "Layers three to six are the parvocellular layers, which receive input from P cells.",
          "start": 88.338,
          "end": 93.578
        },
        {
          "text": "When it comes to the eyes, layers one, four and six receive the contralateral nasal hemiretina.",
          "start": 93.618,
          "end": 99.991
        },
        {
          "text": "Layers two, three and five, by contrast, receive the ipsilateral temporal hemiretina.",
          "start": 100.125,
          "end": 105.658
        },
        {
          "text": "As for where these layers send their output, each channel lands in its own place in the primary visual cortex.",
          "start": 105.698,
          "end": 112.058
        },
        {
          "text": "The magnocellular layers, one and two, project to layer four C alpha.",
          "start": 112.098,
          "end": 116.698
        },
        {
          "text": "The parvocellular layers, three to six, project to layer four C beta.",
          "start": 116.738,
          "end": 121.418
        },
        {
          "text": "And the koniocellular layers project to the blobs of cortical layers two and three.",
          "start": 121.458,
          "end": 126.458
        },
        {
          "text": "Each L G N channel therefore keeps its own cortical address.",
          "start": 126.498,
          "end": 130.525
        },
        {
          "text": "In other words, the P, M and K streams remain segregated on arrival.",
          "start": 130.551,
          "end": 135.778
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Summary",
      "start": 136.281,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "To pull it all together, the lateral geniculate nucleus receives ninety per cent of retinal axons and relays the signal to the visual cortex.",
          "start": 136.281,
          "end": 144.734
        },
        {
          "text": "It also keeps a retinotopic map in which half of the representation is the fovea.",
          "start": 144.788,
          "end": 149.961
        },
        {
          "text": "Its six layers are numbered from ventral to dorsal.",
          "start": 150.041,
          "end": 153.374
        },
        {
          "text": "Layers one and two are magnocellular and take input from M cells, while layers three to six are parvocellular and take input from P cells.",
          "start": 153.508,
          "end": 162.414
        },
        {
          "text": "Layers one, four and six are driven by the contralateral nasal hemiretina, whereas layers two, three and five are driven by the ipsilateral temporal hemiretina.",
          "start": 162.548,
          "end": 172.401
        },
        {
          "text": "Finally, K cells feed the koniocellular layers in between.",
          "start": 172.441,
          "end": 176.788
        },
        {
          "text": "Picture the lateral geniculate nucleus, then, as a relay where each eye and each channel keeps firmly to its own layers.",
          "start": 176.894,
          "end": 184.201
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
