    {"duration":82.102857,"alignment":{"characters":"Now for the structure itself. The L G N is built from six layers of cell bodies, separated by intralaminar layers. These intralaminar layers are also called koniocellular layers, and they take the input from K cells. The six layers are numbered one to six, going from ventral to dorsal. Each layer receives input from one eye only, and from one ganglion cell type only. Because of that, the numbering tells you exactly which input sits where. Layers one and two are the magnocellular layers, which receive input from M cells. Layers three to six are the parvocellular layers, which receive input from P cells. When it comes to the eyes, layers one, four and six receive the contralateral nasal hemiretina. Layers two, three and five, by contrast, receive the ipsilateral temporal hemiretina. As for where these layers send their output, each channel lands in its own place in the primary visual cortex. The magnocellular layers, one and two, project to layer four C alpha. The parvocellular layers, three to six, project to layer four C beta. And the koniocellular layers project to the blobs of cortical layers two and three. Each L G N channel therefore keeps its own cortical address. In other words, the P, M and K streams remain segregated on arrival.","starts":[0,80,160,240,320,347,373,400,480,507,533,560,640,684,729,773,818,862,907,951,996,1040,1160,1220,1280,1380,1480,1580,1680,2240,2320,2347,2373,2400,2480,2560,2640,2800,2880,3040,3120,3200,3280,3360,3376,3392,3408,3424,3440,3520,3580,3640,3700,3760,3840,3920,4000,4080,4240,4267,4293,4320,4347,4373,4400,4480,4520,4560,4680,4710,4740,4770,4800,4880,5160,5216,5272,5328,5384,5440,5480,5520,5560,5600,5669,5737,5806,5874,5943,6011,6080,6160,6200,6240,6320,6380,6440,6500,6560,6640,6720,6780,6840,6900,6960,7000,7040,7120,7267,7413,7560,7707,7853,8000,8040,8080,8128,8176,8224,8272,8320,8400,8420,8440,8460,8480,8560,8640,8700,8760,8820,8880,8920,8960,9160,9193,9227,9260,9293,9327,936